  "content": {
    "id": "p13-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Initially there are 40 mice in a field. Each week, the number of mice in the field increases by 20%. Find the number of mice by the end of:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p13-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "the first week",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "48 mice",
          "worked": "The weekly growth factor is $1+0.20=1.2$.\n$$N_1=40(1.2)=48.$$\nThere are 48 mice.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "the tenth week (round to the nearest whole mouse)",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "248 mice",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}N_{10}&=40(1.2)^{10}\\\\N_{10}&=247.669456896.\\end{align*}$$\nThere are approximately 248 mice.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q9-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "the $n$th week",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$N_n=40(1.2)^n$ mice",
          "worked": "The initial population is 40, and each week multiplies it by $1.2$. After $n$ weeks,\n$$N_n=40(1.2)^n.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p13-q9-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "the hundredth week (give your answer to 2 significant figures; does this answer seem reasonable?)",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Approximately $3.3\\times10^9$ mice.\n\nThis is unreasonable for one field because it could not sustain such a population.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}N_{100}&=40(1.2)^{100}\\\\N_{100}&\\approx3.3\\times10^9.\\end{align*}$$\nThe model predicts more than 3 billion mice. This is unreasonable for one field: limited food and space would prevent indefinite growth at 20% per week.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      }
    ]
  },
